WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
Before I built products,
I studied brains.

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.300
My doctoral training was at MIT,

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.720
in the Harvard-MIT program in
Health Sciences and Technology,

4
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.310
on an NIH training grant.

5
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.220
Across that and my postdoc, at MIT,

6
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.720
Harvard Medical School,
and Beth Israel Deaconess,

7
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.400
I looked at how the systems
linking hearing and movement adapt.

8
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.250
With expertise, and with disorder.

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.010
One study compared white-matter
pathways in singers,

10
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.170
instrumentalists, and people
who don't play at all.

11
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.930
That was published in
Frontiers in Neuroscience.

12
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.180
In 2012, MIT gave me an IDEA² grant,

13
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.350
to develop a new approach to
treating aphasia after a stroke.

14
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.740
And before all that, at the
University of South Florida,

15
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.430
I worked on an EEG brain-computer
interface for people with ALS.

16
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.970
The tools were DTI, fMRI, and EEG.

17
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.070
The question underneath
was always the same.

18
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.550
How does a brain change?

19
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:02.970
And can we help it?

20
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.590
If your work touches any of
this, reach out through the site.
